First, we choose a convenient reference frame.  Let $O$ be the origin.  Let $A$ be the point where $x=1$ and $y=0$.  Let $B$ be the point where $x=1/2$ and $y=\sqrt{3}/2$.  Then $A$ can also be chosen as $A= 1 +0i$ and $B= 1/2 + \sqrt{3}/2 i.$</think>
The problem gives us a vector equation for $P,$ in terms of $t$.  Let's explicitly write out the coordinates for the vector, and simplify:
\begin{align*}
\overrightarrow{OP}&=(2-t)\overrightarrow{OA} + t\overrightarrow{OB}\\
\overrightarrow{OP} &= (2-t)\left(1+0i\right) + t\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) + t\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}i}{2}\right) \\
&= 2-t + \frac{t}{2} + \frac{t\sqrt{3}i}{2} \\
\overrightarrow{OP} &= \frac{t}{2} + 2 - \frac{t}{2} + \frac{t\sqrt{3}i}{2} \\
\overrightarrow{OP} &= 2 + \frac{t\sqrt{3}i}{2} \\
P &= 2 +t\frac{\sqrt{3}i}{2}
\end{align*}
When we graph points on the $trig$ plane, $\overrightarrow{OP}$ has the form $$x + yi = (2 + \frac{t\sqrt{3}i}{2}) = a\cos\theta + bi\sin\theta$$ where $\theta$ is the angle in degree.  Comparing the real parts of the two expressions for $\overrightarrow{OP},$ we have $$\frac{t}{2}=a\cos\theta \quad \Rightarrow \quad a = \frac{t}{2\cos\theta}.$$ Similarly, we have $$\frac{t}{2} = b\sin\theta \Rightarrow y = \frac{t}{2\sin\theta}.$$ Now we know that the minimum distance between $P$ and $A$ will occur when $P$ is directly to the right of $A.$  This situation comes from the diagram below, where we see that $\theta = 0.$
Now we can simplify and compute:
\begin{align*}
| \overrightarrow{AP} | &= | (\sqrt{3}) - 1 | \\
&= \sqrt{ (\sqrt{3}-1)^2 + 0^2} \\
&= \sqrt{3+1-2\sqrt{3}} \\
&= \sqrt{4-2\sqrt{3}}
\end{align*}
